# crados

The filesystem is bytes on a block device, the process table is bytes in RAM,
and every program in /bin is CRX machine code fetched through a page table.

## How a command runs

    timer interrupt
      -> scheduler picks a ready process (round robin, quantum 5)
      -> CPU fetches an instruction through the page table
      -> a sys instruction traps into the kernel

execve reads the inode, follows its block pointers, copies the image into
page frames, and only then starts the CPU. Watch it in dmesg.

## Filesystem

sda is the system disk, in the way C: is the system disk on Windows. It
holds the root tree and every program that ships with the OS, and it is a
real ext2 volume: export it from the Storage panel and e2fsck, debugfs or
mount -o loop on Linux will read it as-is.

    /bin        system programs, installed on sda at power-on
    /usr/man    these manuals
    /root       root's home, and the example sources
    /home       home directories created by useradd
    /mnt        mount point for /dev/sdb
    /tmp        scratch
    /usr/bin    where your own programs go

At power-on the machine asks where the system disk comes from: the copy saved
in IndexedDB, an exported .img, or a brand-new disk with the factory system on
it. Whatever you pick, the disk is written back to IndexedDB as you work.

## First steps

On the first root login, set a password when prompted. After logging in:

    ls -l /bin
    cat count.s
    as count.s -o count
    ./count
    ps

## Experiments

1. Scheduling. Run 'count 30 a &' then 'count 30 b'. The output
   interleaves because the scheduler preempts each process.
2. Blocking. Run 'sleep 8 &' then 'ps'. The sleeper is BLOCK, not
   consuming CPU.
3. Zombies. Run 'sleep 60 &', then 'kill <pid>', then 'ps'. It stays as
   <defunct> until its parent reaps it.
4. Out of memory. Run 'sleep 100 &' repeatedly until fork fails.
5. Accounts. Run 'useradd alice' to create a locked account, then
   'passwd alice' to set her password. After 'exit', log in as alice.
   'users' displays account names, UIDs and permissions. Dropping 'l'
   locks console login. Only root may useradd, passwd other accounts,
   chperm or chown.
6. Permissions. A non-root shell cannot kill root's processes:
   start 'sleep 60 &' as root, then try 'kill <pid>' as alice —
   EPERM until she is granted the 'k' letter.
7. Redirection. Run 'echo hi > /tmp/a' then 'cat /tmp/a'.

## Accounts and permission letters

The account table lives in `/etc/passwd`, one line per account:

    root:0:$2b$12$…:lmbka        name:uid:bcrypt hash:perms

New passwords use bcrypt with cost 12 and a fresh random salt per account.
Passwords must contain at least 6 Unicode characters. The account file is
mode 0600. `-` marks first-time initialization and `!` marks a locked
account. The factory root account must set its password at first login;
accounts created by `useradd` stay locked until root sets a password.
`userdel` removes accounts, `passwd` changes passwords, and `users` only
prints non-sensitive account data. Non-root password changes require the
current password. Legacy numeric hashes can still authenticate for migration:
console login requires an immediate password replacement, and `passwd` always
writes a bcrypt hash. Disk images are not encrypted; anyone who obtains an image
can attempt offline guesses, so use a unique passphrase.

Each account carries a set of permission letters:

    l   login    the account may start a console session
    m   mount    may mount and unmount block devices
    b   block    may read and write raw block devices
    k   kill     may signal processes it does not own
    a   admin    its login session runs with effective uid 0

uid 0 (root) implicitly holds every letter. Dropping 'l' from any other
account locks it out: the console refuses to start its shell. 'chperm
<name> <letters>' rewrites the letter set, e.g. 'chperm alice lmbka'
makes alice an admin. Only root may useradd, userdel, chperm, chown, or
change another account's password. 'su <name>' switches user inside a
session; root needs no password, everyone else types the target's.

## Manuals

    man man        this catalog, and how the pages are stored
    man asm        instruction set, assembler, syscalls
    man storage    disks and the on-disk format
    man inspect    memory, the process table, registers
    man script     shell scripts and the #! mechanism

Chinese versions: man README.zh, man asm.zh, and so on.
